.TH PLOT 2
.SH NAME
plot \- command language for live plots
.SH SYNOPSIS
.EX
include "plot.m";
plot := load Plot Plot->PATH;

p := plot->new(image, font);
p.cmd("colour foreground 16r20272cff\n" +
      "table history time a b -capacity 1200\n" +
      "view phase 40 20 600 430\n" +
      "scale phase x 0 1.5\n" +
      "scale phase y 0 1.5 reverse\n" +
      "axis phase x B grid\n" +
      "axis phase y A grid\n" +
      "line phase history x b y a colour foreground width 2");
p.cmd("history append 0 0.25 0.5");

updates := plot->run(p);
updates <-= ref Plot->Plotmsg(
    "history append 0.1 0.27 0.52  0.2 0.30 0.55",
    nil);
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Plot
interprets a small grammar for live plotting.  Applications declare named
tables and colours, views, linear scales, axes,
and line, point, or vector marks.  Tables are fixed-capacity O(1) ring buffers;
logical rows remain ordered from oldest to newest.
.PP
A command string can contain several newline-separated commands, allowing a
complete plot declaration to be installed with one call.  Like a Tk widget,
each declared table becomes a named command.  Its
.B append
and
.B clear
operations accept the table's natural arity, so table arity remains data in
the language rather than becoming a family of Limbo functions or array-valued
calls.
.PP
An
.B append
command can contain several complete rows.  The number of values must be a
non-zero multiple of the table's column count.  Batching amortizes parsing and
channel synchronization when samples arrive quickly.
.PP
.B run
starts an optional command receiver and returns its input channel.  Producers
send
.B Plotmsg
values containing the same commands accepted by
.BR cmd .
If the reply channel is non-nil, the receiver returns the command's error
string after applying it; a nil string denotes success.  A nil reply channel
makes the message asynchronous.
.PP
The receiver serializes command mutations, but it does not draw or flush the
display.  The window-owning process remains responsible for calling
.B draw
at its chosen display rate.  Once a plot has concurrent producers, mutations
should all pass through the command channel; synchronous queries and drawing
must be coordinated by the owner to avoid observing a partial update.
.PP
The plotting language owns scaling, reversed screen coordinates, nice numeric
ticks, grid lines, mappings, clipping, and rendering.  It does not evaluate
data transformations or differential equations.  Applications and numerical
modules publish already-computed columns.
.PP
.B invert
maps a pointer position in a named view back to data coordinates.
.SS "Declarative panel layout"
.EX
p.cmd("content margin 45 35 18 58\en" +
      "split content x phase 1 time 1 gutter 54\en" +
      "view phase phase\en" +
      "view time time");
.EE
Alongside literal pixel bounds, views can be laid out declaratively \(em
the same idea as a Tk geometry manager, applied to plot panels instead of
widgets: describe the split wanted, not the pixel arithmetic.  Recomputed
from
.B p.image.r
on every call, so it stays correct across a live window resize with no
extra bookkeeping.
.TP
.BI "content margin " l " " t " " r " " b
Registers a rect named
.B content
as the current image's rectangle, shrunk by the given margins.
.TP
.BI "rect " name " " x0 " " y0 " " x1 " " y1
Registers
.I name
as a rect with the given literal bounds \(em for a caller that already
has exact pixel bounds (e.g. because they are also needed directly in
Limbo, for label placement past what the plot itself draws) and only
wants to avoid hand-formatting a
.B view
command's four integers.
.TP
.BI "split " source " x|y " name " " w " [" name " " w " ...] gutter " g
Splits the named rect
.I source
along the given axis into named rects, proportional to each
.IR w ,
separated by
.I g
pixels.
.TP
.BI "view " name " " rect
As well as the original
.BI "view " name " " x0 " " y0 " " x1 " " y1
form, a view can take a previously registered rect's bounds directly by
name, from either
.B content
/
.B split
or
.BR rect .
